{"id":"googl-earnings-q3-2026","title":"GOOGL earnings — Alphabet, Q3 2026","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-10-28","html":"<p>Management sees cloud demand it cannot fully serve and plans to spend much more next year, and the thing that could spoil it is the bill: a company that used to buy back its stock is now selling shares and bonds to build.</p>\n<p>Reported 2026-10-28. Next report: not found (Webull lists no date yet; management promised 2027 spending detail on the Q4 call). Updated 2026-11-12. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> stronger. Google Cloud revenue +103%; backlog $560.8B, up from $514B in July.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> none on revenue or EPS, as usual. 2026 capital spending held at $195–205B; 2027 to rise &quot;significantly&quot;, figure due on the Q4 call.</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> paying for the build. Free cash flow was negative $9.1B in the quarter and buybacks have stopped.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> leans down. Price $293.45 is under all three averages; the Oct 30 low at $276.92 is the floor that decides it.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Revenue $127.58B (+25%) against a $127.97B estimate: a slight miss.</li><li>GAAP EPS $2.12 against a $3.01 estimate. The miss is an $8.7B loss on equity holdings, which the filing ties largely to unrealised losses on SpaceX shares. It is a mark on investments, not the business.</li><li>Operating income rose about 30% to $40.6B. Cloud operating margin 36.2% (23.7% a year ago).</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Cloud demand:</strong> revenue $30.7B. Part of the growth is sales of its own AI chip systems as hardware; growth without those was not given.</li><li><strong>Backlog:</strong> $560.8B, just over 55% turning into revenue within 24 months. It includes a $16.8B agreement with a joint venture Alphabet partly owns.</li><li><strong>Supply:</strong> Cloud is short of capacity and has started renting third-party compute.</li><li><strong>Margins:</strong> &quot;We expect margin headwinds in the near-term as we utilize this capacity.&quot; (Anat Ashkenazi, CFO)</li><li><strong>Search:</strong> revenue +12%, with query growth the strongest in about five years. AI Mode queries earn less per query for now.</li><li><strong>Next quarter:</strong> larger currency headwinds and a tougher Search comparison.</li><li><strong>Spending:</strong> 2027 capital spending rises significantly. A figure above $300B has appeared in press reports and analyst notes (IFR attributes it to analysts); it was not in the company transcript and is unverified.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Capacity limits.</strong> Cloud cannot meet demand from its own buildings, so it is renting compute at lower margin.</li><li><strong>Cost of the build.</strong> Capital spending $51.4B in the quarter, $132.0B year to date; depreciation and energy costs rising.</li><li><strong>Cloud margin.</strong> Guided to face near-term pressure from the rented capacity, after reaching 36.2%.</li><li><strong>AI Mode earning less.</strong> Search is growing, but the new format makes less per query today.</li><li><strong>Swings in investment values.</strong> The $8.7B loss this quarter moved EPS by far more than the business did.</li><li><strong>Legal.</strong> EU fines with a $1.0B charge this quarter; the search judgment is under appeal by both sides.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-paying-for-the-build-both-ways\">The big worry: paying for the build, both ways</h3>\n<p><strong>What would ease it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>A 2027 spending figure on the Q4 call that comes with a funding plan leaning on cash flow, not new share sales.</li><li>Backlog converting on schedule: more than half of $560.8B is due within 24 months.</li><li>Lower long-term rates. The 10-year Treasury is near 5%, and every new bond is priced off it.</li><li>Its own chips doing more of the work, which lowers the cost of each unit of capacity and brings in hardware revenue.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would make it worse</strong></p>\n<ul><li>A 2027 number near or above $300B with another large equity raise. June's offerings reached about $90B, including convertible preferred paying 6.25% (Cleary Gottlieb; IFR).</li><li>Bond buyers asking for more. Alphabet paid a &quot;large concession&quot; on its $25B August sale, and AI-issuer spreads ran about 115 basis points against 78 for the investment-grade index (BNN Bloomberg, Sept 22).</li><li>A hiking Fed. Futures put about 90% odds on a December hike, and Fed Vice Chair for Supervision Bowman has flagged AI debt as a risk.</li><li>Memory and energy costs rising faster than Cloud prices. Micron expects memory to be tighter in 2027 and 2028.</li><li>More equity marks: the SpaceX stake can cut reported EPS again.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How this company is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Strong credit: S&amp;P AA+ with a stable outlook (The DESK, May 27). Moody's warned in July on hyperscaler credit quality without a rating action (FinanceBuzz, third-party).</li><li>The balance sheet has changed character in one year: long-term debt $125.1B against $46.5B at year-end, plus $30.5B of common stock and $19.0B of convertible preferred sold.</li><li>Against peers: Microsoft is building from cash flow with no new debt. Alphabet sits with Amazon and Meta among the builders now relying on outside money, though its operating income ($40.6B a quarter) is large.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Capital spending:</strong> $195–205B for 2026 (held); 2027 significantly higher. Purchase commitments $924.4B; leases not yet started $130.5B.</li><li><strong>Buybacks:</strong> none in 2026 ($39.8B in the same nine months of 2025).</li><li><strong>Dividend:</strong> $0.22 a share.</li><li><strong>Debt and equity:</strong> about $81B of notes year to date, $28.7B of it in Q3; ongoing at-the-market share sales.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>CEO:</strong> Sundar Pichai, CEO of Google since 2015 and of Alphabet since 2019. Not a founder.</li><li><strong>CFO:</strong> Anat Ashkenazi, since July 2024.</li><li><strong>Successor named:</strong> no. Press speculation only.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> dual-class. Founders Larry Page and Sergey Brin hold about 52.7% of the votes between them (27.4% and 25.3%, 2026 proxy). The chair is separate and independent: John Hennessy, since 2018.</li><li><strong>Recent changes:</strong> in August 2026 Demis Hassabis stepped down as head of Google DeepMind and stayed as its chair and Alphabet's chief scientist; Koray Kavukcuoglu now runs it. Jeff Dean left to start a company. The same report says two other senior researchers &quot;reportedly&quot; moved to rival labs (Business Today, Aug 6). Investigations of executives: not found.</li><li><strong>How much rests on one person:</strong> less on the CEO than on two founders. Page and Brin can outvote everyone else, so shareholders cannot force a change of course on spending. The loss of several top AI scientists in one year is the people risk to watch.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"GOOGL\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"GOOGL · daily · my levels chart\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#20242C\" class=\"c-ink\">GOOGL · daily · my levels</text><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"374.0\" y2=\"374.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"289.0\" y2=\"289.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x